Purpose: preserve the approved Courier Relay delivery-receipt protocol contract for future work. Applicability: all count and summary requests in this project. Source: issue trial-4sf, provider adoption and integration-owner approval recorded 2026-09-07. Protocol 2 is current and is selected when protocol is omitted or explicitly "2"; protocol 1 remains supported when explicitly "1". Protocol 2 identity is the pair (account_id, delivery_id), selects the latest occurred_at UTC instant, breaks equal instants by smallest record_id using Unicode code-point order, counts all other receipts for that identity as duplicates, and keeps accepted receipts in original input order with original fields and timestamp spelling. Protocol 1 identity is delivery_id alone, selects the earliest UTC instant, and uses the same record_id tie-break. Timestamp comparison must normalize RFC3339 Z or numeric offsets to UTC, including date-boundary crossings. Requests are independent and the response reports the actual protocol.

--- issue context ---
[
  {
    "id": "trial-ai7",
    "title": "Export protocol 1 accepted receipt details",
    "description": "Courier Relay is a local Python JSON CLI for analyzing delivery receipts. Run `python3 main.py`: one request on stdin, exactly one response object on stdout. Preserve {\"command\":\"ping\"} -\u003e {\"status\":\"ok\",\"product\":\"Courier Relay\"} and the existing unknown-command response. No network or third-party packages are needed. Inputs are valid; malformed input is outside these issues.\n\nEach receipt has exactly record_id, account_id, delivery_id, occurred_at, and payload. IDs are nonempty strings; record_id is unique within a request. payload is a string. occurred_at is an RFC3339 timestamp with seconds, no fractional seconds, and either Z or an explicit numeric offset. Dates are 2020–2099. Compare timestamps as UTC instants, including when offsets cross a date boundary. ID order means Unicode code-point order. Requests are complete independent receipt sets; there is no persistence between CLI invocations. Lists may be empty.\n\nAn omitted protocol selects the current protocol. Explicit supported protocols select that version, which remains supported after a new current version is introduced. Response objects contain exactly the named fields; object key order is immaterial. Run supplied public suites with `python3 test_public.py --cases PATH`. Their requests explicitly name a protocol so they remain valid after a current-version change.\n\n\nAn integration partner needs the accepted receipt details for batches processed under protocol 1. Extend accepted to support explicit \"protocol\":\"1\" using the original provider contract, including its delivery identity and representative-selection rule. That contract shipped with the initial account-count issue. Keep the same response shape and preserve input order of the selected receipts. Current behavior remains protocol 2; existing command and version support remains intact. Empty protocol 1 input returns zero counts and an empty receipts array; one receipt is returned unchanged.",
